<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Inventions That Changed the World</h1><p class="path-tagline">From the wheel and cuneiform to printing, steam power and the electric telegraph.</p><p class="path-intro">The oldest indirect evidence of wheeled movement dates from before 4000 BCE, and the speakers of Sumerian developed cuneiform writing around 3200 BCE. These stops follow inventions from those beginnings through paper, the magnetic compass and movable type, all developed in China, to Gutenberg's press in fifteenth-century Europe. They continue with the new instruments of the Scientific Revolution and with the steam engine, made practical for industry by James Watt's separate condenser, and end with the telegraph of the nineteenth century. Where the entries name the makers, they are stops of their own.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/wheel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wheel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Term · before 4000 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Wheel</span><span class="path-step-why">Miniature clay wheels from north of the Black Sea, from before 4000 BCE, are the oldest indirect evidence of wheeled movement; the spoked wheel later allowed swifter war chariots.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A wheel is a rotating component, usually circular, that turns on an axle; the oldest indirect evidence of wheeled movement, miniature clay wheels found north of the Black Sea, dates from before 4000 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/sumerian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 3200 BCE – c. 80 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Sumerian</span><span class="path-step-why">Speakers of Sumerian, with Egyptian one of the two earliest written languages, developed cuneiform around 3200 BCE, at first to keep accounts, on tablets written at Uruk.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Sumerian language was the language of ancient Sumer, one of the earliest written languages.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/cai-lun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-lun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 62–121</span><span class="path-step-name">Cai Lun</span><span class="path-step-why">In 105 CE this court eunuch of the Eastern Han presented Emperor He with paper made from tree bark, hemp, old rags and fishing nets, cheaper than silk and lighter than bamboo.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Cai Lun was a Chinese court eunuch of the Eastern Han dynasty who, as director of the imperial workshops, presented an improved method of making paper to Emperor He in 105 CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Magnetic compass</span><span class="path-step-why">Developed in China, the magnetised needle was used for navigation at sea under the Song Dynasty, aided maritime trade and later transformed navigation worldwide.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The magnetic compass is a navigational instrument that uses a magnetised needle to indicate direction.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bi-sheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · d. 1051</span><span class="path-step-name">Bi Sheng</span><span class="path-step-why">Around 1040 CE this Song Dynasty artisan invented movable type printing with ceramic characters, a method recorded by the scholar Shen Kuo, about four centuries before Gutenberg.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Bi Sheng was a Chinese artisan of the Song Dynasty credited with inventing movable type printing using ceramic characters around 1040 CE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1400–1468</span><span class="path-step-name">Johannes Gutenberg</span><span class="path-step-why">In the fifteenth century Johannes Gutenberg developed printing with movable metal type in Europe, and his press transformed the spread of knowledge there.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Johannes Gutenberg was a fifteenth-century German craftsman who developed printing with movable metal type in Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/scientific-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1543 CE – 1687 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Scientific Revolution</span><span class="path-step-why">From Copernicus in 1543 to Newton in 1687, European scholars joined mathematics, measurement and experiment and used new instruments such as the telescope, microscope and air pump.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Scientific Revolution was the period, roughly the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, in which new methods of observation, experiment and mathematics transformed European understanding of nature.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steam-engine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Term · 1606</span><span class="path-step-name">Steam engine</span><span class="path-step-why">Jerónimo de Ayanz patented a steam-powered mine pump in 1606, and Thomas Newcomen's engine of about 1712 was the first commercially successful one to transmit continuous power.</span><span class="path-step-lede">A steam engine is a heat engine that does mechanical work with steam, usually by pushing a piston in a cylinder; the Spanish inventor Jerónimo de Ayanz patented the first steam-powered water pump, for draining mines, in 1606.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/james-watt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-watt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1736–1819</span><span class="path-step-name">James Watt</span><span class="path-step-why">James Watt's separate condenser, patented in 1769, greatly reduced fuel use, and with Matthew Boulton at Birmingham from 1775 he built engines that drove mills and pumped mines.</span><span class="path-step-lede">James Watt was a Scottish inventor and instrument maker whose improvements made the steam engine practical for industry.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telegraph.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Telegraph</span><span class="path-step-why">Developed in the nineteenth century, the telegraph sent messages by electrical signals along wires, and submarine cables linked Britain with India, Australia and other parts of its empire.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The telegraph was a system for transmitting messages over long distances by electrical signals along wires, developed in the nineteenth century.</span></span></a></li></ol>
